  "response_text": "While printf is normally used to print output to a console, it is a part of the stdio.h library. This entire library relies on helper functions to interact with the hardware, meaning printf can\u2019t directly interact with hardware by itself. The __io_putchar uses a function HAL_URT_Transmit that \u201cputs\u201d a character into the specified output channel (in this case it is UART2, a peripheral set up by the HAL used to print from the MCU to the PC), and is repeatedly called by printf to print all the characters in the string passed to it. Finally, because the function works using the HAL, the underlying hardware can be changed without needing to change the user code. The HAL provides a generic base to interact with many different types of hardware, which allows this function to still be applicable across many different devices. Without such a function (and without the HAL in general), a hardware-specific version would be needed for each different device, significantly and negatively impacting portability and adaptability.\\n",
